INSERT INTO `rag_chunks` VALUES (9,8,0,' AI-Driven Crime Risk Forecasting for Sustainable Governance: A Multi-Method District-Level Analysis of India Using IPC Crime Data and Census Socio-Economic Indicators Vinod G R Dept. of CSE, PES University Bangalore, India pes1ug23cs692@pesu.pes.edu Sachin L Dept. of CSE, PES University Bangalore, India pes1ug23cs496@pesu.pes.edu Vikas S Dept. of CSE, PES University Bangalore, India pes1ug23cs685@pesu.pes.edu Akash K Dept. of CSE, PES University Bangalore, India pes1ug23cs042@pesu.pes.edu Dr. Divyashree N Dept. of CSE, PES University Bangalore, India divyashreen@pes.edu Abstract—Government crime data in India remains largely retro- spective, limiting proactive governance aligned with SDG 16.1. With 640+ districts and 1.4 billion people, India lacks a systematic district- level predictive intelligence layer. This paper addresses that gap using NCRB IPC records (2001–2012, 416 districts, 30 crime categories) merged with Census 2011 socio-economic indicators (118 features), applying five complementary methods: ARIMA time-series forecast- ing, supervised ML (Linear Regression, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting), SHAP explainability, K-Means and DBSCAN spatial clus- tering, and a composite Crime Risk Index (CRI). A strict temporal train/test split ensures realistic evaluation on held-out 2012 data. Gra- dient Boosting achievesR 2 = 0.983(MAE = 83 crimes/district) on the 2012 test set; ARIMA yields a median absolute error of 8.5%. Gender-based violence surged 58.4% (2001–2012) versus only 18.6% total IPC growth—3.14×faster. Poverty ratio is the dominant struc- tural OLS predictor (β = 33,340,p < 0.001). DBSCAN flags 181 anomalous districts for targeted intervention. Top-5 high-CRI districts (Dibang Valley, Narayanpur, Alirajpur, Jhabua, Pratapgarh) cluster in central and north-eastern India, revealing convergent zones of low literacy, high poverty, and infrastructure deficit. Keywords: predictive policing, crime forecasting, ARIMA, gradient boosting, SHAP, DBSCAN, K-Means, Crime Risk Index, India NCRB, gender-based violence, SDG 16. I. INTRODUCTION A. Problem Domain India’s National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) publishes district-level IPC crime statistics annually, covering 27 states/UTs and more than 640 districts. Yet the current policing model remains predominantly reactive: resources are allocated retrospectively based on last year’s totals rather than forward- looking risk assessments. At this scale— 1.4 billion people, 30 IPC crime categories, and 12-year longitudinal records—purely manual analysis is infeasible. United Nations Sustainable De- velopment Goal 16 (Peace, Justice, and Strong Institutions), specifically Target 16.1, calls for a significant reduction in all forms of violence. Meeting this target requires predictive intel- ligence, not just post-hoc tallies. Existing systems suffer from three critical gaps: (i) no district-level ML forecasting integrating socio-economic cen- sus data; (ii) no composite risk index enabling cross-district policy triage; and (iii) no explainability layer to make AI rec- ommendations auditable for public governance. This paper directly addresses all three gaps. B. Key Contributions This work makes five principal contributions: 1. The first comprehensive multi-method crime forecasting system validated at the India district level across 12 years. 2.A temporal ML framework with engineered lag features achieving R 2 = 0.983 on held-out 2012 data. 3.A SHAP explainability layer enabling transparent, auditable AI for crime governance. 4. A composite CRI combining five socio-economic dimen- sions with quantile-calibrated risk labels deployable in an- nual planning cycles. 5.Discovery of a gender','2026-07-26 09:29:59'),(10,8,1,'policy triage; and (iii) no explainability layer to make AI rec- ommendations auditable for public governance. This paper directly addresses all three gaps. B. Key Contributions This work makes five principal contributions: 1. The first comprehensive multi-method crime forecasting system validated at the India district level across 12 years. 2.A temporal ML framework with engineered lag features achieving R 2 = 0.983 on held-out 2012 data. 3.A SHAP explainability layer enabling transparent, auditable AI for crime governance. 4. A composite CRI combining five socio-economic dimen- sions with quantile-calibrated risk labels deployable in an- nual planning cycles. 5.Discovery of a gender violence surge disproportionate to total crime growth (+58.4% vs. +18.6%), signalling a struc- tural shift in India’s crime landscape. II. LITERATURE SURVEY Table 1 summarises relevant prior work. The dominant gap across all cited papers is the absence of a unified framework combining time-series forecasting, supervised ML, explainabil- ity, and a composite risk index validated on India’s district-level administrative geography. III. DATASET AND TOOLS A. Dataset 1 — NCRB IPC Crime Data The primary dataset is sourced from India’s NCRB and covers 2001–2012 (12 years). After removing 185 non-geographic Table 1: Literature Survey #AuthorsDatasetYearMethodologyKey ResultGap [1] Gorr & HarriesPittsburgh PD2003ARIMA Crime forecastable 1-month ahead No socio-economic in- tegration [2] Chainey et al.UK Police2008KDE hotspots Hotspotaccuracy 90% No temporal ML [3] Iqbal et al.Pakistan crime2013Na ̈ ıve Bayes80% classification No regression fore- casting [4] Perry et al.US cities2013 Pred. policing re- view Overview of toolsNo India context [5] McClendon& Meghanathan US crime2015LSTM RMSE 12% better than ARIMA No explainability [6] Cozens & LoveInternational2016CPTED review Environmental de- sign reduces crime No ML [7] Khanna & BhattNCRB India2017Trend analysis+15%decadal growth No district-ML [8] Kaur & SinghIndia census2018OLS regressionLiteracy significantNo lag features [9] Devi et al.Indian states2019K-Means4 crime clustersNo supervised ML [10] Gupta & SharmaIndia GBV2020Descriptive statsGBV +40% 2010– 2019 No predictive model [11] Bappee et al.Montreal2018RF + spatialR 2 = 0.81No SHAP [12] Yi et al.Chicago2020XGBoost + SHAPTop features identi- fied No India data [13] Wu et al.Smart city2021GB + DBSCANOutlierdistricts flagged No CRI [14] UN SDGGlobal2015Policy frameworkTarget 16.1 violence reduction No quantitative model [15] Sharma et al.India NCRB2021Panel regressionPoverty significantNo clustering or CRI entries (railway zones, GRP, RPF, and TOTAL rows), 9,017 raw rows remain spanning 416 unique districts across 27 states/UTs. Thirty IPC crime categories are recorded per district-year, in- cluding Murder, Rape, Dacoity, Theft, Dowry Deaths, and Cruelty by Husband. B. Dataset 2 — Census 2011 Socio-Economic Indicators Census 2011 provides 640 districts×118 features. Twenty- four features were selected for analysis: Population, Liter- ate, Male/Female Workers, Cultivator Workers, SC/ST counts, household infrastructure (electric lighting, latrine, tapwater), poverty (Power Parity<Rs. 45,000), and educational attain- ment. This dataset is merged with the crime data on dis- trict+state keys after uppercase standardisation, yielding 4,832 district-year observations× 61 features. C. Tools Python 3.11 was used throughout.Key libraries: pandas 2.x,numpy,scikit-learn 1.8(ML, clus- tering, PCA, scaling),statsmodels 0.14(OLS, ARIMA, ADF test),SHAP 0.51(TreeExplainer),NetworkX(graph analysis), and matplotlib/seaborn (visualisation). IV. PROPOSED METHODOLOGY The end-to-end pipeline flows through ten steps: Raw','2026-07-26 09:30:00'),(11,8,2,'B. Dataset 2 — Census 2011 Socio-Economic Indicators Census 2011 provides 640 districts×118 features. Twenty- four features were selected for analysis: Population, Liter- ate, Male/Female Workers, Cultivator Workers, SC/ST counts, household infrastructure (electric lighting, latrine, tapwater), poverty (Power Parity<Rs. 45,000), and educational attain- ment. This dataset is merged with the crime data on dis- trict+state keys after uppercase standardisation, yielding 4,832 district-year observations× 61 features. C. Tools Python 3.11 was used throughout.Key libraries: pandas 2.x,numpy,scikit-learn 1.8(ML, clus- tering, PCA, scaling),statsmodels 0.14(OLS, ARIMA, ADF test),SHAP 0.51(TreeExplainer),NetworkX(graph analysis), and matplotlib/seaborn (visualisation). IV. PROPOSED METHODOLOGY The end-to-end pipeline flows through ten steps: Raw NCRB + Census data→Preprocessing & merge→Feature engineering →EDA→OLS socio-economic regression→ARIMA forecast- ing→Supervised ML (temporal split)→SHAP explainability →Clustering (K-Means + DBSCAN)→Crime Risk Index (CRI) + network analysis. A. Preprocessing and Feature Engineering Column names were lower-cased and space-replaced with underscores.District and state names were upper-cased for join consistency.After merging on district+state, 11 normalised socio-economic ratios were computed (liter- acyrate, workerratio, urbanratio, scstratio, povertyratio, infraindex, youthratio, and others), three crime compos- ites (violent, property, gender), and temporal lag features: prevcrime,prev2crime,crimema3(3-year moving average),prevgender, andyoygrowth(year-on-year % change). B. OLS Cross-Sectional Regression A cross-sectional OLS model was estimated on the 2011 slice (N = 408), deliberately excluding lag features so that socio- economic effect sizes remain uncontaminated by temporal au- tocorrelation. 2 C. ARIMA Time-Series Forecasting ARIMA(1,1,1) was fitted independently to each of the 383 districts with complete 12-year series. The Augmented Dickey- Fuller (ADF) test confirmed non-stationarity at level (p = 0.48) and stationarity after first differencing (p < 0.05). Training used 2001–2011; the 2012 national total served as the one-year- ahead test horizon. D. Supervised ML with Temporal Split Fifteen features were used (year, 6 lag features, 7 socio- economic ratios, population). No random shuffling was ap- plied. Training rows:≤2010 (3,184 obs.), validation: 2011 (408 obs.), test: 2012 (404 obs.). Three models were compared: Linear Regression, Random Forest (200 trees, maxdepth = 12), and Gradient Boosting (200 estimators, depth = 5, lr = 0.05). E. SHAP Explainability TreeExplainer was applied to the trained Random Forest on a 200-sample subset of test observations, generating global summary and bar charts (Figs. 4), quantifying each feature’s marginal contribution to the prediction. F. Spatial Clustering K-Means (k = 5, selected by elbow method; Fig. 5) and DBSCAN (eps = 0.8, minsamples = 4) were applied to the 2011 cross-section on seven StandardScaler-normalised fea- tures: crimerate, literacyrate, workerratio, urbanratio, povertyratio, violentrate, and genderrate. G. Crime Risk Index The CRI is computed as: CRI raw = 0.40 ̃ C+0.20(1− ̃ L)+0.20 ̃ P +0.10 ̃ W +0.10(1− ̃ I) (1) where ̃ C= MinMax-normalised crime rate, ̃ L= literacy rate, ̃ P = poverty ratio, ̃ W= worker ratio, ̃ I= infrastructure index. CRI is scaled to 0–100 and labelled Low (bottom 60%), Medium (60th–80th percentile), High (top 20%). V. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION A. EDA: National Crime Trends Fig. 1 shows the dual-axis national trend. Total IPC crimes grew from 1,123,254 in 2001 to 1,331,680 in 2012 (+18.6%). Over','2026-07-26 09:30:01'),(12,8,3,'urbanratio, povertyratio, violentrate, and genderrate. G. Crime Risk Index The CRI is computed as: CRI raw = 0.40 ̃ C+0.20(1− ̃ L)+0.20 ̃ P +0.10 ̃ W +0.10(1− ̃ I) (1) where ̃ C= MinMax-normalised crime rate, ̃ L= literacy rate, ̃ P = poverty ratio, ̃ W= worker ratio, ̃ I= infrastructure index. CRI is scaled to 0–100 and labelled Low (bottom 60%), Medium (60th–80th percentile), High (top 20%). V. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION A. EDA: National Crime Trends Fig. 1 shows the dual-axis national trend. Total IPC crimes grew from 1,123,254 in 2001 to 1,331,680 in 2012 (+18.6%). Over the same period, gender-based violence cases rose from 80,881 to 128,096 (+58.4%)—3.14×faster than the overall crime rate. This divergence constitutes a structural shift, not merely a proportional increase, with significant implications for SDG 16.1. Figure 1: National IPC crime trend and gender violence trend 2001– 2012 (dual y-axis, right axis in thousands). B. Socio-Economic Drivers (OLS) The cross-sectional OLS model (Table 2) explains 34.3% of variance in district crime rate (R 2 = 0.3427, adjustedR 2 = 0.3329). The strongest significant predictor is povertyratio (β = 33,340,p < 0.001), indicating that a unit increase in the poverty ratio is associated with 33,340 additional crimes per 100,000 population, ceteris paribus. workerratio (β = 359.8, p < 0.001) and urbanratio (β = 204.9,p < 0.001) are also significant. Counterintuitively, literacyrate shows a positive correlation (r = +0.250) with crime rate—explained by higher FIR reporting rates in literate urban districts (a reporting effect, not a causal one). The scstratio is significantly negative (β = −156.2,p < 0.001), consistent with under-reporting or community cohesion in scheduled-community districts. Fig. 2 displays the full correlation heatmap. Figure 2: Socio-economic×crime-rate correlation matrix (2011 cross-section). Table 2: OLS Cross-Sectional Regression Results (N = 408, 2011) VariableCoeff. p-valueSig. Constant55.70.681ns literacyrate59.60.413ns workerratio359.8 <0.001*** urban ratio204.9 <0.001*** povertyratio33,340.4 <0.001*** scstratio−156.2 <0.001*** youthratio−162.50.260ns R 2 = 0.343 Adj. R 2 = 0.333 C. ML Model Comparison Table 3 compares the three supervised models. Gradient Boosting achieves the best test performance:R 2 = 0.983, MAE = 83 crimes/district, RMSE = 399. Fig. 3 shows actual-vs- predicted scatter plots and residual diagnostics. GB residuals are tightly centred around zero with minimal heteroscedastic- ity, confirming strong generalisation. SHAP analysis (Fig. 4) 3 reveals thatprevcrime(mean|SHAP| = 2031) alone domi- nates predictions, confirming strong year-to-year persistence in district crime levels—a finding that directly motivates ARIMA as a complementary interpretable baseline. The povertyratio (rank 4, SHAP = 7.25) and workerratio (rank 5, SHAP = 6.86) confirm the structural drivers identified by OLS. Table 3: ML Model Performance (Temporal Split) ModelVal R 2 Val MAE Test R 2 Test MAE Test RMSE Lin. Reg.0.9512660.966251563 Rand. Forest 0.974940.97399508 Grad. Boost 0.991690.98383399 MAE & RMSE in absolute crime counts per district. Figure 3: Actual vs. predicted (top) and residual plots (bottom) for Linear Regression, Random Forest, and Gradient Boosting on 2012 test data. Figure 4: SHAP beeswarm summary plot (Random Forest, n = 200 test samples). Colour indicates feature value (red','2026-07-26 09:30:01'),(13,8,4,'complementary interpretable baseline. The povertyratio (rank 4, SHAP = 7.25) and workerratio (rank 5, SHAP = 6.86) confirm the structural drivers identified by OLS. Table 3: ML Model Performance (Temporal Split) ModelVal R 2 Val MAE Test R 2 Test MAE Test RMSE Lin. Reg.0.9512660.966251563 Rand. Forest 0.974940.97399508 Grad. Boost 0.991690.98383399 MAE & RMSE in absolute crime counts per district. Figure 3: Actual vs. predicted (top) and residual plots (bottom) for Linear Regression, Random Forest, and Gradient Boosting on 2012 test data. Figure 4: SHAP beeswarm summary plot (Random Forest, n = 200 test samples). Colour indicates feature value (red = high). D. ARIMA Forecasting ARIMA(1,1,1) was fitted to 383 districts. Across those districts, the mean absolute error is 366 crimes (RMSE = 771), corre- sponding to a mean absolute error % of 11.8% and a median absolute error % of 8.5%. The low median error confirms that ARIMA is a reliable single-district planner even when a handful of high-growth districts inflate the mean. E. Spatial Clustering and CRI The K-Means elbow curve (Fig. 5) justifiesk = 5. The five clus- ters span crime rates from 108 (Low, 134 districts) to 1,124 (Ex- treme outlier, 1 district). DBSCAN (eps = 0.8, minsamples = 4) identified only 2 dense clusters and 181 outlier districts with atypical crime profiles—prime candidates for targeted policy intervention. Figure 5: K-Means elbow curve: inertia vs. number of clusters. The inflection at k = 5 justifies the chosen value. Fig. 6 presents the top-20 districts by CRI (2012) and the national risk distribution. The top-5 high-risk districts are listed in Table 4. All five are in central or north-eastern India, con- verging zones of high poverty, low literacy, and infrastructure deficit. The 23.2% High-risk share (pie chart) implies roughly 94 districts require immediate policy attention—an actionable output for SDG 16.1 planning. Figure 6: Top-20 districts by CRI score (2012) and national risk distribution (pie chart). Table 4: Top-5 High-Risk Districts by CRI (2012) StateDistrictCRIRisk Arunachal PradeshDibang Valley68.65High ChhattisgarhNarayanpur45.23High Madhya PradeshAlirajpur41.73High Madhya PradeshJhabua39.54High RajasthanPratapgarh38.25High VI. CONCLUSION This paper presented a ten-step AI-driven crime forecasting framework applied to 4,832 district-year observations spanning 4 India’s 416 districts from 2001 to 2012. Gradient Boosting achieved the highest accuracy (R 2 = 0.983, MAE = 83), while ARIMA provided an interpretable per-district baseline (median error 8.5%). The most critical societal finding is the gender violence surge of +58.4% versus +18.6% total IPC growth— a structural divergence demanding dedicated policy attention aligned with SDG 16.1. Poverty emerged as the dominant structural driver (β = 33,340,p < 0.001), confirmed inde- pendently by OLS and SHAP. The CRI (Eq. 1) operationalises these findings into a deployable district triage tool, flagging 181 DBSCAN-anomalous districts and identifying Dibang Valley, Narayanpur, Alirajpur, Jhabua, and Pratapgarh as the highest- risk districts for 2012. Limitations: The dataset ends in 2012 and Census 2011 is static for all years. No police capacity variable is included. DBSCAN is sensitive to the eps parameter. Results are observa- tional; causal inference requires instrumental variable','2026-07-26 09:30:02'),(14,8,5,'growth— a structural divergence demanding dedicated policy attention aligned with SDG 16.1. Poverty emerged as the dominant structural driver (β = 33,340,p < 0.001), confirmed inde- pendently by OLS and SHAP. The CRI (Eq. 1) operationalises these findings into a deployable district triage tool, flagging 181 DBSCAN-anomalous districts and identifying Dibang Valley, Narayanpur, Alirajpur, Jhabua, and Pratapgarh as the highest- risk districts for 2012. Limitations: The dataset ends in 2012 and Census 2011 is static for all years. No police capacity variable is included. DBSCAN is sensitive to the eps parameter. Results are observa- tional; causal inference requires instrumental variable methods. Future work: LSTM/Transformer time-series models; real- time NCRB integration; police station density as an additional feature; causal inference; and satellite/mobile data fusion. ACKNOWLEDGEMENT The authors gratefully acknowledge the National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) of India for the IPC crime dataset and the Office of the Registrar General & Census Commissioner of India for Census 2011 data available via data.gov.in. APPENDIX I: FEATURE ENGINEERING FORMULAS literacy rate = literate/population femaleliteracy = femaleliterate/(female + 1) workerratio = workers/population female workerr = femaleworkers/(female + 1) agriworkerr = (cult. + agri.)/(workers + 1) crimerate = totalipc/population× 100,000 urbanratio = urbanHH/(HH + 1) scstratio = (SC + ST)/(population + 1) youth ratio = age 0–29 /(population + 1) infraindex = (elec + latrine + tapwater)/(HH×3 + 1) povertyratio = pp <45k /(HH + 1) CRI Weight Rationale: ComponentWeightRationale Crime rate ( ̃ C)0.40Primary outcome—direct in- tensity 1−Literacy ( ̃ L)0.20Persistent structural risk Poverty ( ̃ P )0.20Strongest OLS predictor Worker ratio ( ̃ W )0.10Employment as protective factor 1−Infra ( ̃ I )0.10Infrastructure deficit ampli- fies risk REFERENCES [1]W. Gorr and R. Harries, “Introduction to crime forecasting,” Int. J. Forecasting, vol. 19, no. 4, pp. 551–555, 2003. [2]S. Chainey, L. Tompson, and S. Uhlig, “The utility of hotspot mapping for predicting spatial patterns of crime,” Security J., vol. 21, no. 1–2, pp. 4–28, 2008. [3]R. Iqbal, A. Murad, A. Mushtaq, P. Haque, and M. Shad, “An empirical study of classification algorithms for crime prediction,” J. Intell. Learning Syst. Appl., vol. 5, no. 3, 2013. [4]W. Perry, B. McInnis, C. Price, S. Smith, and J. Hollywood, Predictive Policing: The Role of Crime Forecasting in Law Enforcement Operations. RAND Corporation, 2013. [5]L. McClendon and N. Meghanathan, “Using machine learning algorithms to analyze crime data,” Machine Learning Appl.: Int. J., vol. 2, no. 1, pp. 1–12, 2015. [6]P. Cozens and T. Love, “A review and current status of crime prevention through environmental design (CPTED),” J. Planning Literature, vol. 30, no. 4, pp. 393–412, 2015. [7]A. Khanna and P. Bhatt, “Crime analysis on NCRB data using data mining techniques,” in Proc. Int. Conf. Inventive Systems and Control (ICISC), 2017, pp. 1–5. [8] N. Kaur and S. Singh, “Effect of socio-economic factors on crime in India: A statistical analysis,” Int. J. Applied Soc. Sci., vol. 5, no. 7–8, pp. 614–620, 2018. [9] T. Devi, A. Nandal, and S. Dhiman, “Predicting crime in Indian states using K-Means clustering,” Int. J.','2026-07-26 09:30:03'),(15,8,6,'[6]P. Cozens and T. Love, “A review and current status of crime prevention through environmental design (CPTED),” J. Planning Literature, vol. 30, no. 4, pp. 393–412, 2015. [7]A. Khanna and P. Bhatt, “Crime analysis on NCRB data using data mining techniques,” in Proc. Int. Conf. Inventive Systems and Control (ICISC), 2017, pp. 1–5. [8] N. Kaur and S. Singh, “Effect of socio-economic factors on crime in India: A statistical analysis,” Int. J. Applied Soc. Sci., vol. 5, no. 7–8, pp. 614–620, 2018. [9] T. Devi, A. Nandal, 