 is Live-Variable Analysis?\n●Live variable analysis is based on the following definition -\n○For a variable x and program point p determine if the value of x at p \ncan still be used along some path starting at p.\n○If yes, x is live at p.\n○If not, x is dead at p.\n\n●In the given illustration -\n○At point p the x variable is live if there is a path\nfrom p\n0 \nwhere x is used.\n○\n0\nBeyond p\nx\n towards p\n2\nthe value of x is no longer \nneeded and is dead.\n\nCompiler Design \nLiveness\n●Livenessisassociatedwithedgesofcontrolflowgraph,not\nnodes (statements).\n\nCompiler Design \nApplications\n●Register Allocation - If a variable is dead at a given point p, we can reuse its \nstorage, i.e, the register it occupies (if any).\n●Dead code elimination - If variable x is not live after an assignment x = ..., \nthen the assignment is redundant and can be deleted as dead code.\n\nCompiler Design \nData-flow values\nLive variable Analysis algorithm requires the computationofthe following \ndata-flow values -\n\n●use[n] : set of variables used by Node n\n●def [n] : set of variables defined by Node n\n●in[n] : variables live on entry to Node n\n●out[n] : variables live on exit from Node n\n\nCompiler Design \nData-flow equations\nThe data-flow values are then used to compute IN and OUT sets for each basic \nblock B using the data flow equations-\n\nOUT [B] = ∪S is a successor of B ( IN[S] )\n\nIN [B] = use[B] ∪ ( OUT[B] - def[B] ) \n\nCompiler Design\nLive-Variable Analysis Algorithm\n\nCompiler Design \nExample 1\nComputeLivenessInformation \nfor the following CFG -\n\nCompiler Design \nExample 1 - Solution\nStep  2  -  Compute  use \nand def for each basic \nblock.\nuse[n] : set of variables used \nby Node n\ndef [n] : set of variables\ndefined by Node n\n\nCompiler Design \nDataflow equations\nStep 3 - Compute IN and OUT for each basic block\nOUT [B] = ∪ \nS is a successor of B\n( IN[S] )\nIN [B] = use[B] ∪ ( OUT[B] - def[B] \n)\n\nCompiler Design \nExample 1 Solution\n\nCompiler Design \nExample 2\nComputeLivenessInformation \nfor the following CFG -\n\nCompiler Design \nExample 2 - Solution\nStep 2 - Compute use and def for \neach basic block.\n\nCompiler Design \nExample 2 - Solution\nStep3-ComputeIN\nand OUT for each basic \nblock\n\nCompiler Design \nExample 3\nComputeLivenessInformation \nfor the following CFG -\n\nCompiler Design \nExample 3 - Solution\n\nCompiler Design \nExample 4\nComputeLivenessInformation \nfor the following CFG -\n\nCompiler Design \nExample 4 - Solution\n\nCompiler Design \nExample 4 - Solution\n\nCompiler Design \nExample 5\nCompute Liveness \nInformation for the \nfollowing CFG -\n\nStep 2 - Compute use \nand def for each basic \nblock.\nCompiler Design \nExample 2 - Solution\n\nCompiler Design \nExample 2 - Solution\n\nPreet Kanwal\nDepartment of Computer Science & Engineering\npreetkanwal@pes.edu\nTHANK \nYOU','2026-07-26 07:04:20'),(4,6,'\n\nAI-Driven Crime Risk Forecasting for Sustainable Governance: A\nMulti-Method District-Level Analysis of India Using IPC Crime Data\nand Census Socio-Economic Indicators\nVinod G R\nDept. of CSE, PES University\nBangalore, India\npes1ug23cs692@pesu.pes.edu\nSachin L\nDept. of CSE, PES University\nBangalore, India\npes1ug23cs496@pesu.pes.edu\nVikas S\nDept. of CSE, PES University\nBangalore, India\npes1ug23cs685@pesu.pes.edu\nAkash K\nDept. of CSE, PES University\nBangalore, India\npes1ug23cs042@pesu.pes.edu\nDr. Divyashree N\nDept. of CSE, PES University\nBangalore, India\ndivyashreen@pes.edu\nAbstract—Government crime data in India remains largely retro-\nspective, limiting proactive governance aligned with SDG 16.1. With\n640+ districts and 1.4 billion people, India lacks a systematic district-\nlevel predictive intelligence layer. This paper addresses that gap using\nNCRB IPC records (2001–2012, 416 districts, 30 crime categories)\nmerged with Census 2011 socio-economic indicators (118 features),\napplying five complementary methods: ARIMA time-series forecast-\ning, supervised ML (Linear Regression, Random Forest, Gradient\nBoosting), SHAP explainability, K-Means and DBSCAN spatial clus-\ntering, and a composite Crime Risk Index (CRI). A strict temporal\ntrain/test split ensures realistic evaluation on held-out 2012 data. Gra-\ndient Boosting achievesR\n2\n= 0.983(MAE = 83 crimes/district) on\nthe 2012 test set; ARIMA yields a median absolute error of 8.5%.\nGender-based violence surged 58.4% (2001–2012) versus only 18.6%\ntotal IPC growth—3.14×faster. Poverty ratio is the dominant struc-\ntural OLS predictor (β = 33,340,p < 0.001).  DBSCAN flags 181\nanomalous districts for targeted intervention. Top-5 high-CRI districts\n(Dibang Valley, Narayanpur, Alirajpur, Jhabua, Pratapgarh) cluster\nin central and north-eastern India, revealing convergent zones of low\nliteracy, high poverty, and infrastructure deficit.\nKeywords: predictive policing, crime forecasting, ARIMA, gradient\nboosting, SHAP, DBSCAN, K-Means, Crime Risk Index, India NCRB,\ngender-based violence, SDG 16.\nI.  INTRODUCTION\nA. Problem Domain\nIndia’s  National  Crime  Records  Bureau  (NCRB)  publishes\ndistrict-level   IPC   crime   statistics   annually,   covering   27\nstates/UTs and more than 640 districts. Yet the current policing\nmodel remains predominantly reactive: resources are allocated\nretrospectively based on last year’s totals rather than forward-\nlooking risk assessments. At this scale— 1.4 billion people, 30\nIPC crime categories, and 12-year longitudinal records—purely\nmanual analysis is infeasible. United Nations Sustainable De-\nvelopment Goal 16 (Peace, Justice, and Strong Institutions),\nspecifically Target 16.1, calls for a significant reduction in all\nforms of violence. Meeting this target requires predictive intel-\nligence, not just post-hoc tallies.\nExisting  systems  suffer  from  three  critical  gaps:   (i)  no\ndistrict-level ML forecasting integrating socio-economic cen-\nsus data; (ii) no composite risk index enabling cross-district\npolicy triage; and (iii) no explainability layer to make AI rec-\nommendations auditable for public governance.  This paper\ndirectly addresses all three gaps.\nB. Key Contributions\nThis work makes five principal contributions:\n1.\nThe first comprehensive multi-method crime forecasting\nsystem validated at the India district level across 12 years.\n2.A temporal ML framework with engineered lag features\nachieving R\n2\n= 0.983 on held-out 2012 data.\n3.A SHAP explainability layer enabling transparent, auditable\nAI for crime governance.\n4.\nA composite CRI combining five socio-economic dimen-\nsions with quantile-calibrated risk labels deployable in an-\nnual planning cycles.\n5.Discovery of a gender violence surge disproportionate to\ntotal crime growth (+58.4% vs. +18.6%), signalling a struc-\ntural shift in India’s crime landscape.\nII.  LITERATURE SURVEY\nTable 1 summarises relevant prior work.  The dominant gap\nacross all cited papers is the absence of a unified framework\ncombining time-series forecasting, supervised ML, explainabil-\nity, and a composite risk index validated on India’s district-level\nadministrative geography.\nIII.  DATASET AND TOOLS\nA. Dataset 1 — NCRB IPC Crime Data\nThe primary dataset is sourced from India’s NCRB and covers\n2001–2012 (12 years).  After removing 185 non-geographic\n\nTable 1: Literature Survey\n#AuthorsDatasetYearMethodologyKey ResultGap\n[1]\nGorr & HarriesPittsburgh PD2003ARIMA\nCrime  forecastable\n1-month ahead\nNo socio-economic in-\ntegration\n[2]\nChainey et al.UK Police2008KDE hotspots\nHotspotaccuracy\n90%\nNo temporal ML\n[3]\nIqbal et al.Pakistan crime2013Na\n ̈\nıve Bayes80% classification\nNo   regression   fore-\ncasting\n[4]\nPerry et al.US cities2013\nPred.  policing  re-\nview\nOverview of toolsNo India context\n[5]\nMcClendon&\nMeghanathan\nUS crime2015LSTM\nRMSE  12%  better\nthan ARIMA\nNo explainability\n[6]\nCozens & LoveInternational2016CPTED review\nEnvironmental   de-\nsign reduces crime\nNo ML\n[7]\nKhanna & BhattNCRB India2017Trend analysis+15%decadal\ngrowth\nNo district-ML\n[8]\nKaur & SinghIndia census2018OLS regressionLiteracy significantNo lag features\n[9]\nDevi et al.Indian states2019K-Means4 crime clustersNo supervised ML\n[10]\nGupta & SharmaIndia GBV2020Descriptive statsGBV  +40%  2010–\n2019\nNo predictive model\n[11]\nBappee et al.Montreal2018RF + spatialR\n2\n= 0.81No SHAP\n[12]\nYi et al.Chicago2020XGBoost + SHAPTop features identi-\nfied\nNo India data\n[13]\nWu et al.Smart city2021GB + DBSCANOutlierdistricts\nflagged\nNo CRI\n[14]\nUN SDGGlobal2015Policy frameworkTarget 16.1 violence\nreduction\nNo quantitative model\n[15]\nSharma et al.India NCRB2021Panel regressionPoverty significantNo clustering or CRI\nentries (railway zones, GRP, RPF, and TOTAL rows), 9,017 raw\nrows remain spanning 416 unique districts across 27 states/UTs.\nThirty IPC crime categories are recorded per district-year, in-\ncluding  Murder,  Rape,  Dacoity,  Theft,  Dowry  Deaths,  and\nCruelty by Husband.\nB. Dataset 2 — Census 2011 Socio-Economic Indicators\nCensus 2011 provides 640 districts×118 features.  Twenty-\nfour  features  were  selected  for  analysis:  Population,  Liter-\nate, Male/Female Workers, Cultivator Workers, SC/ST counts,\nhousehold infrastructure (electric lighting, latrine, tapwater),\npoverty (Power Parity<Rs. 45,000), and educational attain-\nment.   This  dataset  is  merged  with  the  crime  data  on  dis-\ntrict+state keys after uppercase standardisation, yielding 4,832\ndistrict-year observations× 61 features.\nC. Tools\nPython    3.11    was    used    throughout.Key    libraries:\npandas 2.x,numpy,scikit-learn 1.8(ML,  clus-\ntering, PCA, scaling),statsmodels 0.14(OLS, ARIMA,\nADF test),SHAP 0.51(TreeExplainer),NetworkX(graph\nanalysis), and matplotlib/seaborn (visualisation).\nIV.  PROPOSED METHODOLOGY\nThe end-to-end pipeline flows through ten steps: Raw NCRB +\nCensus data→Preprocessing & merge→Feature engineering\n→EDA→OLS socio-economic regression→ARIMA forecast-\ning→Supervised ML (temporal split)→SHAP explainability\n→Clustering (K-Means + DBSCAN)→Crime Risk Index\n(CRI) + network analysis.\nA. Preprocessing and Feature Engineering\nColumn  names  were  lower-cased  and  space-replaced  with\nunderscores.District  and  state  names  were  upper-cased\nfor  join  consistency.After  merging  on  district+state,  11\nnormalised   socio-economic   ratios   were   computed   (liter-\nacyrate, workerratio, urbanratio, scstratio, povertyratio,\ninfraindex,  youthratio,  and  others),  three  crime  compos-\nites  (violent,  property,  gender),  and  temporal  lag  features:\nprevcrime,prev2crime,crimema3(3-year moving\naverage),prevgender, andyoygrowth(year-on-year %\nchange).\nB. OLS Cross-Sectional Regression\nA cross-sectional OLS model was estimated on the 2011 slice\n(N = 408), deliberately excluding lag features so that socio-\neconomic effect sizes remain uncontaminated by temporal au-\ntocorrelation.\n2\n\nC. ARIMA Time-Series Forecasting\nARIMA(1,1,1) was fitted independently to each of the 383\ndistricts with complete 12-year series. The Augmented Dickey-\nFuller (ADF) test confirmed non-stationarity at level (p = 0.48)\nand stationarity after first differencing (p < 0.05).  Training\nused 2001–2011; the 2012 national total served as the one-year-\nahead test horizon.\nD. Supervised ML with Temporal Split\nFifteen  features  were  used  (year,  6  lag  features,  7  socio-\neconomic ratios, population). No random shuffling was ap-\nplied.  Training rows:≤2010 (3,184 obs.), validation:  2011\n(408 obs.), test: 2012 (404 obs.). Three models were compared:\nLinear Regression, Random Forest (200 trees, maxdepth = 12),\nand Gradient Boosting (200 estimators, depth = 5, lr = 0.05).\nE. SHAP Explainability\nTreeExplainer was applied to the trained Random Forest on\na 200-sample subset of test observations,  generating global\nsummary and bar charts (Figs. 4), quantifying each feature’s\nmarginal contribution to the prediction.\nF. Spatial Clustering\nK-Means  (k = 5,  selected  by  elbow  method;  Fig.  5)  and\nDBSCAN  (eps = 0.8,  minsamples = 4)  were  applied  to  the\n2011 cross-section on seven StandardScaler-normalised fea-\ntures:    crimerate,  literacyrate,  workerratio,  urbanratio,\npovertyratio, violentrate, and genderrate.\nG. Crime Risk Index\nThe CRI is computed as:\nCRI\nraw\n= 0.40\n ̃\nC+0.20(1−\n ̃\nL)+0.20\n ̃\nP +0.10\n ̃\nW +0.10(1−\n ̃\nI)\n(1)\nwhere\n ̃\nC= MinMax-normalised crime rate,\n ̃\nL= literacy rate,\n ̃\nP\n= poverty ratio,\n ̃\nW= worker ratio,\n ̃\nI= infrastructure index. CRI\nis scaled to 0–100 and labelled Low (bottom 60%), Medium\n(60th–80th percentile), High (top 20%).\nV.  RESULTS AND DISCUSSION\nA. EDA: National Crime Trends\nFig. 1 shows the dual-axis national trend.  Total IPC crimes\ngrew from 1,123,254 in 2001 to 1,331,680 in 2012 (+18.6%).\nOver the same period, gender-based violence cases rose from\n80,881 to 128,096 (+58.4%)—3.14×faster than the overall\ncrime rate.  This divergence constitutes a structural shift, not\nmerely a proportional increase, with significant implications\nfor SDG 16.1.\nFigure 1: National IPC crime trend and gender violence trend 2001–\n2012 (dual y-axis, right axis in thousands).\nB. Socio-Economic Drivers (OLS)\nThe cross-sectional OLS model (Table 2) explains 34.3% of\nvariance in district crime rate (R\n2\n= 0.3427, adjustedR\n2\n=\n0.3329).  The strongest significant predictor is povertyratio\n(β = 33,340,p < 0.001), indicating that a unit increase in the\npoverty ratio is associated with 33,340 additional crimes per\n100,000 population, ceteris paribus. workerratio (β = 359.8,\np < 0.001) and urbanratio (β = 204.9,p < 0.001) are also\nsignificant.  Counterintuitively, literacyrate shows a positive\ncorrelation (r = +0.250) with crime rate—explained by higher\nFIR reporting rates in literate urban districts (a reporting effect,\nnot a causal one).   The scstratio is significantly negative\n(β = −156.2,p < 0.001), consistent with under-reporting or\ncommunity cohesion in scheduled-community districts. Fig. 2\ndisplays the full correlation heatmap.\nFigure 2:  Socio-economic×crime-rate correlation matrix (2011\ncross-section).\nTable 2: OLS Cross-Sectional Regression Results (N = 408, 2011)\nVariableCoeff.   p-valueSig.\nConstant55.70.681ns\nliteracyrate59.60.413ns\nworkerratio359.8   <0.001***\nurban\nratio204.9   <0.001***\npovertyratio33,340.4  <0.001***\nscstratio−156.2  <0.001***\nyouthratio−162.50.260ns\nR\n2\n= 0.343    Adj. R\n2\n= 0.333\nC. ML Model Comparison\nTable  3  compares  the  three  supervised  models.   Gradient\nBoosting achieves the best test performance:R\n2\n= 0.983,\nMAE = 83 crimes/district, RMSE = 399. Fig. 3 shows actual-vs-\npredicted scatter plots and residual diagnostics. GB residuals\nare tightly centred around zero with minimal heteroscedastic-\nity, confirming strong generalisation. SHAP analysis (Fig. 4)\n3\n\nreveals thatprevcrime(mean|SHAP| = 2031) alone domi-\nnates predictions, confirming strong year-to-year persistence in\ndistrict crime levels—a finding that directly motivates ARIMA\nas a complementary interpretable baseline. The povertyratio\n(rank 4, SHAP = 7.25) and workerratio (rank 5, SHAP = 6.86)\nconfirm the structural drivers identified by OLS.\nTable 3: ML Model Performance (Temporal Split)\nModelVal\nR\n2\nVal\nMAE\nTest\nR\n2\nTest\nMAE\nTest\nRMSE\nLin. Reg.0.9512660.966251563\nRand. Forest    0.974940.97399508\nGrad. Boost    0.991690.98383399\nMAE & RMSE in absolute crime counts per district.\nFigure 3: Actual vs. predicted (top) and residual plots (bottom) for\nLinear Regression, Random Forest, and Gradient Boosting on 2012\ntest data.\nFigure 4: SHAP beeswarm summary plot (Random Forest, n = 200\ntest samples). Colour indicates feature value (red = high).\nD. ARIMA Forecasting\nARIMA(1,1,1) was fitted to 383 districts. Across those districts,\nthe mean absolute error is 366 crimes (RMSE = 771), corre-\nsponding to a mean absolute error % of 11.8% and a median\nabsolute error % of 8.5%.  The low median error confirms\nthat ARIMA is a reliable single-district planner even when a\nhandful of high-growth districts inflate the mean.\nE. Spatial Clustering and CRI\nThe K-Means elbow curve (Fig. 5) justifiesk = 5. The five clus-\nters span crime rates from 108 (Low, 134 districts) to 1,124 (Ex-\ntreme outlier, 1 district). DBSCAN (eps = 0.8, minsamples = 4)\nidentified only 2 dense clusters and 181 outlier districts with\natypical crime profiles—prime candidates for targeted policy\nintervention.\nFigure 5: K-Means elbow curve: inertia vs. number of clusters. The\ninflection at k = 5 justifies the chosen value.\nFig. 6 presents the top-20 districts by CRI (2012) and the\nnational risk distribution. The top-5 high-risk districts are listed\nin Table 4.  All five are in central or north-eastern India, con-\nverging zones of high poverty, low literacy, and infrastructure\ndeficit. The 23.2% High-risk share (pie chart) implies roughly\n94 districts require immediate policy attention—an actionable\noutput for SDG 16.1 planning.\nFigure 6:  Top-20 districts by CRI score (2012) and national risk\ndistribution (pie chart).\nTable 4: Top-5 High-Risk Districts by CRI (2012)\nStateDistrictCRIRisk\nArunachal PradeshDibang Valley68.65High\nChhattisgarhNarayanpur45.23High\nMadhya PradeshAlirajpur41.73High\nMadhya PradeshJhabua39.54High\nRajasthanPratapgarh38.25High\nVI.  CONCLUSION\nThis paper presented a ten-step AI-driven crime forecasting\nframework applied to 4,832 district-year observations spanning\n4\n\nIndia’s 416 districts from 2001 to 2012.  Gradient Boosting\nachieved the highest accuracy (R\n2\n= 0.983, MAE = 83), while\nARIMA provided an interpretable per-district baseline (median\nerror 8.5%).  The most critical societal finding is the gender\nviolence surge of +58.4% versus +18.6% total IPC growth—\na structural divergence demanding dedicated policy attention\naligned with SDG 16.1.   Poverty emerged as the dominant\nstructural driver (β = 33,340,p < 0.001), confirmed inde-\npendently by OLS and SHAP. The CRI (Eq. 1) operationalises\nthese findings into a deployable district triage tool, flagging 181\nDBSCAN-anomalous districts and identifying Dibang Valley,\nNarayanpur, Alirajpur, Jhabua, and Pratapgarh as the highest-\nrisk districts for 2012.\nLimitations: The dataset ends in 2012 and Census 2011 is\nstatic for all years.  No police capacity variable is included.\nDBSCAN is sensitive to the eps parameter. Results are observa-\ntional; causal inference requires instrumental variable methods.\nFuture work: LSTM/Transformer time-series models; real-\ntime NCRB integration; police station density as an additional\nfeature; causal inference; and satellite/mobile data fusion.\nACKNOWLEDGEMENT\nThe  authors  gratefully  acknowledge  the  National  Crime\nRecords Bureau (NCRB) of India for the IPC crime dataset and\nthe Office of the Registrar General & Census Commissioner of\nIndia for Census 2011 data available via data.gov.in.\nAPPENDIX I: FEATURE ENGINEERING FORMULAS\nliteracy\nrate = literate/population\nfemaleliteracy = femaleliterate/(female + 1)\nworkerratio = workers/population\nfemale\nworkerr = femaleworkers/(female + 1)\nagriworkerr = (cult. + agri.)/(workers + 1)\ncrimerate = totalipc/population× 100,000\nurbanratio = urbanHH/(HH + 1)\nscstratio = (SC + ST)/(population + 1)\nyouth\nratio = age\n0–29\n/(population + 1)\ninfraindex = (elec + latrine + tapwater)/(HH×3 + 1)\npovertyratio = pp\n<45k\n/(HH + 1)\nCRI Weight Rationale:\nComponentWeightRationale\nCrime rate (\n ̃\nC)0.40Primary outcome—direct in-\ntensity\n1−Literacy (\n ̃\nL)0.20Persistent structural risk\nPoverty (\n ̃\nP )0.20Strongest OLS predictor\nWorker ratio (\n ̃\nW )0.10Employment  as  protective\nfactor\n1−Infra (\n ̃\nI )0.10Infrastructure deficit ampli-\nfies risk\nREFERENCES\n[1]W. Gorr and R. Harries, “Introduction to crime forecasting,” Int.\nJ. Forecasting, vol. 19, no. 4, pp. 551–555, 2003.\n[2]S. Chainey, L. Tompson, and S. Uhlig, “The utility of hotspot\nmapping for predicting spatial patterns of crime,” Security J.,\nvol. 21, no. 1–2, pp. 4–28, 2008.\n[3]R. Iqbal, A. Murad, A. Mushtaq, P. Haque, and M. Shad, “An\nempirical study of classification algorithms for crime prediction,”\nJ. Intell. Learning Syst. Appl., vol. 5, no. 3, 2013.\n[4]W. Perry, B. McInnis, C. Price, S. Smith, and J. Hollywood,\nPredictive Policing: The Role of Crime Forecasting in Law\nEnforcement Operations. RAND Corporation, 2013.\n[5]L. McClendon and N. Meghanathan, “Using machine learning\nalgorithms to analyze crime data,” Machine Learning Appl.: Int.\nJ., vol. 2, no. 1, pp. 1–12, 2015.\n[6]P. Cozens and T. Love, “A review and current status of crime\nprevention through environmental design (CPTED),” J. Planning\nLiterature, vol. 30, no. 4, pp. 393–412, 2015.\n[7]A. Khanna and P. Bhatt, “Crime analysis on NCRB data using\ndata mining techniques,” in Proc. Int. Conf. Inventive Systems\nand Control (ICISC), 2017, pp. 1–5.\n[8]\nN. Kaur and S. Singh,  “Effect of socio-economic factors on\ncrime in India: A statistical analysis,” Int. J. Applied Soc. Sci.,\nvol. 5, no. 7–8, pp. 614–620, 2018.\n[9]\nT. Devi, A. Nandal, and S. Dhiman, “Predicting crime in Indian\nstates using K-Means clustering,” Int. J. Recent Technol. Eng.,\nvol. 8, no. 2S3, pp. 912–918, 2019.\n[10]\nS. Gupta and R. Sharma, “Trends in gender-based violence in\nIndia: An analysis of NCRB data 2010–2019,” Indian J. Crimi-\nnology, vol. 48, no. 1, pp. 23–39, 2020.\n[11]F. K. Bappee, A. Junior, and A. Matwin, “Predicting crime using\nspatial features,” in Proc. Canadian AI 2018, LNAI 10832, 2018,\npp. 367–373.\n[12]D.  Yi,  S.  Liu,  and  R.  Ji,  “Applying  SHAP  to  explain  crime\nprediction models,” IEEE Access, vol. 8, pp. 171169–171178,\n2020.\n[13]\nQ. Wu, H. Chen, and Z. Li, “Urban crime pattern detection using\ngradient boosting and DBSCAN,” Sustainability, vol. 13, no. 15,\np. 8426, 2021.\n[14]\nUnited Nations, “Transforming our world: The 2030 agenda for\nsustainable development,” A/RES/70/1, UN General Assembly,\n2015.\n[15]\nD. Sharma, K. Gupta, and M. Tiwari, “Panel data analysis of\ncrime determinants across Indian states,” J. Policy Modeling,\nvol. 43, no. 2, pp. 458–475, 2021.\n[16]L. E. Cohen and M. Felson, “Social change and crime rate trends:\nA routine activity approach,” American Sociol. Rev.,  vol. 44,\nno. 4, pp. 588–608, 1979.\n[17]R. K. Merton, “Social structure and anomie,” American Sociol.\nRev., vol. 3, no. 5, pp. 672–682, 1938.\n[18]C. R. Shaw and H. D. McKay, Juvenile Delinquency and Urban\nAreas. Univ. Chicago Press, 1942.\n[19]T. Chen and C. Guestrin, “XGBoost: A scalable tree boosting\nsystem,” in Proc. 22nd ACM SIGKDD, 2016, pp. 785–794.\n[20]L. Breiman, “Random forests,” Machine Learning, vol. 45, no. 1,\npp. 5–32, 2001.\n[21]S. M. Lundberg and S.-I. Lee, “A unified approach to interpreting\nmodel predictions,” in Advances in Neural Information Process-\ning Systems 30, 2017, pp. 4765–4774.\n5\n\n[22]M. Ester, H.-P. Kriegel, J. Sander, and X. Xu, “A density-based\nalgorithm for discovering clusters in large spatial databases,” in\nProc. 2nd Int. Conf. KDD, 1996, pp. 226–231.\n[23]J. MacQueen, “Some methods for classification and analysis of\nmultivariate observations,” in Proc. 5th Berkeley Symp. Math.\nStatist. Prob., 1967, pp. 281–297.\n[24]G. E. P. Box and G. M. Jenkins, Time Series Analysis: Forecast-\ning and Control. Holden-Day, 1970.\n[25]W. Skogan, Disorder and Decline: Crime and the Spiral of\nDecay in American Neighborhoods. Free Press, 1990.\n[26]S. D. Levitt, “Using electoral cycles in police hiring to estimate\nthe effect of police on crime,” American Econ. Rev., vol. 87,\nno. 3, pp. 270–290, 1997.\n[27]National Crime Records Bureau, Crime in India 2012. Ministry\nof Home Affairs, Government of India, New Delhi, 2013.\n[28]Office of the Registrar General & Census Commissioner, Census\nof India 2011: District-Level Data. Government of India, 2012.\n[Online]. Available: https://data.gov.in\n[29]F. Pedregosa et al., “Scikit-learn: Machine learning in Python,”\nJ. Machine Learning Research, vol. 12, pp. 2825–2830, 2011.\n[30]\nS.  Seabold  and  J.  Perktold,  “Statsmodels:  Econometric  and\nstatistical modeling with Python,” in Proc. 9th Python in Science\nConf., 2010, pp. 92–96.\n[31]A. A. Hagberg, D. A. Schult, and P. J. Swart, “Exploring network\nstructure, dynamics, and function using NetworkX,” in Proc. 7th\nPython in Science Conf., 2008, pp. 11–15.\n[32]J. Kleinberg, H. Lakkaraju, J. Leskovec, J. Ludwig, and S. Mul-\nlainathan, “Human decisions and machine predictions,” Q. J.\nEcon., vol. 133, no. 1, pp. 237–293, 2018.\n[33]W. D. Heaven, “Predictive policing algorithms are racist. They\nneed to be dismantled,” MIT Technology Review, Jul. 2020.\n[34]G. O. Mohler, M. B. Short, P. J. Brantingham, F. P. Schoenberg,\nand G. E. Tita, “Self-exciting point process modeling of crime,”\nJ. Amer. Statist. Assoc., vol. 106, no. 493, pp. 100–108, 2011.\n[35]B. Wang, Y. Zhang, and X. Wang, “A review of deep learning\nmethods applied to crime prediction,” IEEE Trans. Comput. Soc.\nSyst., vol. 6, no. 4, pp. 678–689, 2019.\n[36]N. Dash and B. Patra, “Machine learning-based crime prediction\nsystem for Indian districts using NCRB data,” J. Ambient Intell.\nHumanized Comput., vol. 13, pp. 5613–5626, 2022.\n[37]S. Misra and A. Singh, “Socio-economic correlates of crime in\nIndia: Evidence from panel data,” Econ. Political Weekly, vol. 57,\nno. 12, pp. 44–51, 2022.\n[38]UNICEF, “Violence against women and girls: A compendium\nof monitoring and evaluation indicators,” UNICEF, New York,\n2020.\n[39]\nUNDP, “Tackling social norms:  A game changer for gender\ninequalities,” Human Development Perspectives, UNDP, New\nYork, 2020.\n6','2026-07-26 07:25:41'),(6,8,'\n\nAI-Driven Crime Risk Forecasting for Sustainable Governance: A\nMulti-Method District-Level Analysis of India Using IPC Crime Data\nand Census Socio-Economic Indicators\nVinod G R\nDept. of CSE, PES University\nBangalore, India\npes1ug23cs692@pesu.pes.edu\nSachin L\nDept. of CSE, PES University\nBangalore, India\npes1ug23cs496@pesu.pes.edu\nVikas S\nDept. of CSE, PES University\nBangalore, India\npes1ug23cs685@pesu.pes.edu\nAkash K\nDept. of CSE, PES University\nBangalore, India\npes1ug23cs042@pesu.pes.edu\nDr. Divyashree N\nDept. of CSE, PES University\nBangalore, India\ndivyashreen@pes.edu\nAbstract—Government crime data in India remains largely retro-\nspective, limiting proactive governance aligned with SDG 16.1. With\n640+ districts and 1.4 billion people, India lacks a systematic district-\nlevel predictive intelligence layer. This paper addresses that gap using\nNCRB IPC records (2001–2012, 416 districts, 30 crime categories)\nmerged with Census 2011 socio-economic indicators (118 features),\napplying five complementary methods: ARIMA time-series forecast-\ning, supervised ML (Linear Regression, Random Forest, Gradient\nBoosting), SHAP explainability, K-Means and DBSCAN spatial clus-\ntering, and a composite Crime Risk Index (CRI). A strict temporal\ntrain/test split ensures realistic evaluation on held-out 2012 data. Gra-\ndient Boosting achievesR\n2\n= 0.983(MAE = 83 crimes/district) on\nthe 2012 test set; ARIMA yields a median absolute error of 8.5%.\nGender-based violence surged 58.4% (2001–2012) versus only 18.6%\ntotal IPC growth—3.14×faster. Poverty ratio is the dominant struc-\ntural OLS predictor (β = 33,340,p < 0.001).  DBSCAN flags 181\nanomalous districts for targeted intervention. Top-5 high-CRI districts\n(Dibang Valley, Narayanpur, Alirajpur, Jhabua, Pratapgarh) cluster\nin central and north-eastern India, revealing convergent zones of low\nliteracy, high poverty, and infrastructure deficit.\nKeywords: predictive policing, crime forecasting, ARIMA, gradient\nboosting, SHAP, DBSCAN, K-Means, Crime Risk Index, India NCRB,\ngender-based violence, SDG 16.\nI.  INTRODUCTION\nA. Problem Domain\nIndia’s  National  Crime  Records  Bureau  (NCRB)  publishes\ndistrict-level   IPC   crime   statistics   annually,   covering   27\nstates/UTs and more than 640 districts. Yet the current policing\nmodel remains predominantly reactive: resources are allocated\nretrospectively based on last year’s totals rather than forward-\nlooking risk assessments. At this scale— 1.4 billion people, 30\nIPC crime categories, and 12-year longitudinal records—purely\nmanual analysis is infeasible. United Nations Sustainable De-\nvelopment Goal 16 (Peace, Justice, and Strong Institutions),\nspecifically Target 16.1, calls for a significant reduction in all\nforms of violence. Meeting this target requires predictive intel-\nligence, not just post-hoc tallies.\nExisting  systems  suffer  from  three  critical  gaps:   (i)  no\ndistrict-level ML forecasting integrating socio-economic cen-\nsus data; (ii) no composite risk index enabling cross-district\npolicy triage; and (iii) no explainability layer to make AI rec-\nommendations auditable for public governance.  This paper\ndirectly addresses all three gaps.\nB. Key Contributions\nThis work makes five principal contributions:\n1.\nThe first comprehensive multi-method crime forecasting\nsystem validated at the India district level across 12 years.\n2.A temporal ML framework with engineered lag features\nachieving R\n2\n= 0.983 on held-out 2012 data.\n3.A SHAP explainability layer enabling transparent, auditable\nAI for crime governance.\n4.\nA composite CRI combining five socio-economic dimen-\nsions with quantile-calibrated risk labels deployable in an-\nnual planning cycles.\n5.Discovery of a gender violence surge disproportionate to\ntotal crime growth (+58.4% vs. +18.6%), signalling a struc-\ntural shift in India’s crime landscape.\nII.  LITERATURE SURVEY\nTable 1 summarises relevant prior work.  The dominant gap\nacross all cited papers is the absence of a unified framework\ncombining time-series forecasting, supervised ML, explainabil-\nity, and a composite risk index validated on India’s district-level\nadministrative geography.\nIII.  DATASET AND TOOLS\nA. Dataset 1 — NCRB IPC Crime Data\nThe primary dataset is sourced from India’s NCRB and covers\n2001–2012 (12 years).  After removing 185 non-geographic\n\nTable 1: Literature Survey\n#AuthorsDatasetYearMethodologyKey ResultGap\n[1]\nGorr & HarriesPittsburgh PD2003ARIMA\nCrime  forecastable\n1-month ahead\nNo socio-economic in-\ntegration\n[2]\nChainey et al.UK Police2008KDE hotspots\nHotspotaccuracy\n90%\nNo temporal ML\n[3]\nIqbal et al.Pakistan crime2013Na\n ̈\nıve Bayes80% classification\nNo   regression   fore-\ncasting\n[4]\nPerry et al.US cities2013\nPred.  policing  re-\nview\nOverview of toolsNo India context\n[5]\nMcClendon&\nMeghanathan\nUS crime2015LSTM\nRMSE  12%  better\nthan ARIMA\nNo explainability\n[6]\nCozens & LoveInternational2016CPTED review\nEnvironmental   de-\nsign reduces crime\nNo ML\n[7]\nKhanna & BhattNCRB India2017Trend analysis+15%decadal\ngrowth\nNo district-ML\n[8]\nKaur & SinghIndia census2018OLS regressionLiteracy significantNo lag features\n[9]\nDevi et al.Indian states2019K-Means4 crime clustersNo supervised ML\n[10]\nGupta & SharmaIndia GBV2020Descriptive statsGBV  +40%  2010–\n2019\nNo predictive model\n[11]\nBappee et al.Montreal2018RF + spatialR\n2\n= 0.81No SHAP\n[12]\nYi et al.Chicago2020XGBoost + SHAPTop features identi-\nfied\nNo India data\n[13]\nWu et al.Smart city2021GB + DBSCANOutlierdistricts\nflagged\nNo CRI\n[14]\nUN SDGGlobal2015Policy frameworkTarget 16.1 violence\nreduction\nNo quantitative model\n[15]\nSharma et al.India NCRB2021Panel regressionPoverty significantNo clustering or CRI\nentries (railway zones, GRP, RPF, and TOTAL rows), 9,017 raw\nrows remain spanning 416 unique districts across 27 states/UTs.\nThirty IPC crime categories are recorded per district-year, in-\ncluding  Murder,  Rape,  Dacoity,  Theft,  Dowry  Deaths,  and\nCruelty by Husband.\nB. Dataset 2 — Census 2011 Socio-Economic Indicators\nCensus 2011 provides 640 districts×118 features.  Twenty-\nfour  features  were  selected  for  analysis:  Population,  Liter-\nate, Male/Female Workers, Cultivator Workers, SC/ST counts,\nhousehold infrastructure (electric lighting, latrine, tapwater),\npoverty (Power Parity<Rs. 45,000), and educational attain-\nment.   This  dataset  is  merged  with  the  crime  data  on  dis-\ntrict+state keys after uppercase standardisation, yielding 4,832\ndistrict-year observations× 61 features.\nC. Tools\nPython    3.11    was    used    throughout.Key    libraries:\npandas 2.x,numpy,scikit-learn 1.8(ML,  clus-\ntering, PCA, scaling),statsmodels 0.14(OLS, ARIMA,\nADF test),SHAP 0.51(TreeExplainer),NetworkX(graph\nanalysis), and matplotlib/seaborn (visualisation).\nIV.  PROPOSED METHODOLOGY\nThe end-to-end pipeline flows through ten steps: Raw NCRB +\nCensus data→Preprocessing & merge→Feature engineering\n→EDA→OLS socio-economic regression→ARIMA forecast-\ning→Supervised ML (temporal split)→SHAP explainability\n→Clustering (K-Means + DBSCAN)→Crime Risk Index\n(CRI) + network analysis.\nA. Preprocessing and Feature Engineering\nColumn  names  were  lower-cased  and  space-replaced  with\nunderscores.District  and  state  names  were  upper-cased\nfor  join  consistency.After  merging  on  district+state,  11\nnormalised   socio-economic   ratios   were   computed   (liter-\nacyrate, workerratio, urbanratio, scstratio, povertyratio,\ninfraindex,  youthratio,  and  others),  three  crime  compos-\nites  (violent,  property,  gender),  and  temporal  lag  features:\nprevcrime,prev2crime,crimema3(3-year moving\naverage),prevgender, andyoygrowth(year-on-year %\nchange).\nB. OLS Cross-Sectional Regression\nA cross-sectional OLS model was estimated on the 2011 slice\n(N = 408), deliberately excluding lag features so that socio-\neconomic effect sizes remain uncontaminated by temporal au-\ntocorrelation.\n2\n\nC. ARIMA Time-Series Forecasting\nARIMA(1,1,1) was fitted independently to each of the 383\ndistricts with complete 12-year series. The Augmented Dickey-\nFuller (ADF) test confirmed non-stationarity at level (p = 0.48)\nand stationarity after first differencing (p < 0.05).  Training\nused 2001–2011; the 2012 national total served as the one-year-\nahead test horizon.\nD. Supervised ML with Temporal Split\nFifteen  features  were  used  (year,  6  lag  features,  7  socio-\neconomic ratios, population). No random shuffling was ap-\nplied.  Training rows:≤2010 (3,184 obs.), validation:  2011\n(408 obs.), test: 2012 (404 obs.). Three models were compared:\nLinear Regression, Random Forest (200 trees, maxdepth = 12),\nand Gradient Boosting (200 estimators, depth = 5, lr = 0.05).\nE. SHAP Explainability\nTreeExplainer was applied to the trained Random Forest on\na 200-sample subset of test observations,  generating global\nsummary and bar charts (Figs. 4), quantifying each feature’s\nmarginal contribution to the prediction.\nF. Spatial Clustering\nK-Means  (k = 5,  selected  by  elbow  method;  Fig.  5)  and\nDBSCAN  (eps = 0.8,  minsamples = 4)  were  applied  to  the\n2011 cross-section on seven StandardScaler-normalised fea-\ntures:    crimerate,  literacyrate,  workerratio,  urbanratio,\npovertyratio, violentrate, and genderrate.\nG. Crime Risk Index\nThe CRI is computed as:\nCRI\nraw\n= 0.40\n ̃\nC+0.20(1−\n ̃\nL)+0.20\n ̃\nP +0.10\n ̃\nW +0.10(1−\n ̃\nI)\n(1)\nwhere\n ̃\nC= MinMax-normalised crime rate,\n ̃\nL= literacy rate,\n ̃\nP\n= poverty ratio,\n ̃\nW= worker ratio,\n ̃\nI= infrastructure index. CRI\nis scaled to 0–100 and labelled Low (bottom 60%), Medium\n(60th–80th percentile), High (top 20%).\nV.  RESULTS AND DISCUSSION\nA. EDA: National Crime Trends\nFig. 1 shows the dual-axis national trend.  Total IPC crimes\ngrew from 1,123,254 in 2001 to 1,331,680 in 2012 (+18.6%).\nOver the same period, gender-based violence cases rose from\n80,881 to 128,096 (+58.4%)—3.14×faster than the overall\ncrime rate.  This divergence constitutes a structural shift, not\nmerely a proportional increase, with significant implications\nfor SDG 16.1.\nFigure 1: National IPC crime trend and gender violence trend 2001–\n2012 (dual y-axis, right axis in thousands).\nB. Socio-Economic Drivers (OLS)\nThe cross-sectional OLS model (Table 2) explains 34.3% of\nvariance in district crime rate (R\n2\n= 0.3427, adjustedR\n2\n=\n0.3329).  The strongest significant predictor is povertyratio\n(β = 33,340,p < 0.001), indicating that a unit increase in the\npoverty ratio is associated with 33,340 additional crimes per\n100,000 population, ceteris paribus. workerratio (β = 359.8,\np < 0.001) and urbanratio (β = 204.9,p < 0.001) are also\nsignificant.  Counterintuitively, literacyrate shows a positive\ncorrelation (r = +0.250) with crime rate—explained by higher\nFIR reporting rates in literate urban districts (a reporting effect,\nnot a causal one).   The scstratio is significantly negative\n(β = −156.2,p < 0.001), consistent with under-reporting or\ncommunity cohesion in scheduled-community districts. Fig. 2\ndisplays the full correlation heatmap.\nFigure 2:  Socio-economic×crime-rate correlation matrix (2011\ncross-section).\nTable 2: OLS Cross-Sectional Regression Results (N = 408, 2011)\nVariableCoeff.   p-valueSig.\nConstant55.70.681ns\nliteracyrate59.60.413ns\nworkerratio359.8   <0.001***\nurban\nratio204.9   <0.001***\npovertyratio33,340.4  <0.001***\nscstratio−156.2  <0.001***\nyouthratio−162.50.260ns\nR\n2\n= 0.343    Adj. R\n2\n= 0.333\nC. ML Model Comparison\nTable  3  compares  the  three  supervised  models.   Gradient\nBoosting achieves the best test performance:R\n2\n= 0.983,\nMAE = 83 crimes/district, RMSE = 399. Fig. 3 shows actual-vs-\npredicted scatter plots and residual diagnostics. GB residuals\nare tightly centred around zero with minimal heteroscedastic-\nity, confirming strong generalisation. SHAP analysis (Fig. 4)\n3\n\nreveals thatprevcrime(mean|SHAP| = 2031) alone domi-\nnates predictions, confirming strong year-to-year persistence in\ndistrict crime levels—a finding that directly motivates ARIMA\nas a complementary interpretable baseline. The povertyratio\n(rank 4, SHAP = 7.25) and workerratio (rank 5, SHAP = 6.86)\nconfirm the structural drivers identified by OLS.\nTable 3: ML Model Performance (Temporal Split)\nModelVal\nR\n2\nVal\nMAE\nTest\nR\n2\nTest\nMAE\nTest\nRMSE\nLin. Reg.0.9512660.966251563\nRand. Forest    0.974940.97399508\nGrad. Boost    0.991690.98383399\nMAE & RMSE in absolute crime counts per district.\nFigure 3: Actual vs. predicted (top) and residual plots (bottom) for\nLinear Regression, Random Forest, and Gradient Boosting on 2012\ntest data.\nFigure 4: SHAP beeswarm summary plot (Random Forest, n = 200\ntest samples). Colour indicates feature value (red = high).\nD. ARIMA Forecasting\nARIMA(1,1,1) was fitted to 383 districts. Across those districts,\nthe mean absolute error is 366 crimes (RMSE = 771), corre-\nsponding to a mean absolute error % of 11.8% and a median\nabsolute error % of 8.5%.  The low median error confirms\nthat ARIMA is a reliable single-district planner even when a\nhandful of high-growth districts inflate the mean.\nE. Spatial Clustering and CRI\nThe K-Means elbow curve (Fig. 5) justifiesk = 5. The five clus-\nters span crime rates from 108 (Low, 134 districts) to 1,124 (Ex-\ntreme outlier, 1 district). DBSCAN (eps = 0.8, minsamples = 4)\nidentified only 2 dense clusters and 181 outlier districts with\natypical crime profiles—prime candidates for targeted policy\nintervention.\nFigure 5: K-Means elbow curve: inertia vs. number of clusters. The\ninflection at k = 5 justifies the chosen value.\nFig. 6 presents the top-20 districts by CRI (2012) and the\nnational risk distribution. The top-5 high-risk districts are listed\nin Table 4.  All five are in central or north-eastern India, con-\nverging zones of high poverty, low literacy, and infrastructure\ndeficit. The 23.2% High-risk share (pie chart) implies roughly\n94 districts require immediate policy attention—an actionable\noutput for SDG 16.1 planning.\nFigure 6:  Top-20 districts by CRI score (2012) and national risk\ndistribution (pie chart).\nTable 4: Top-5 High-Risk Districts by CRI (2012)\nStateDistrictCRIRisk\nArunachal PradeshDibang Valley68.65High\nChhattisgarhNarayanpur45.23High\nMadhya PradeshAlirajpur41.73High\nMadhya PradeshJhabua39.54High\nRajasthanPratapgarh38.25High\nVI.  CONCLUSION\nThis paper presented a ten-step AI-driven crime forecasting\nframework applied to 4,832 district-year observations spanning\n4\n\nIndia’s 416 districts from 2001 to 2012.  Gradient Boosting\nachieved the highest accuracy (R\n2\n= 0.983, MAE = 83), while\nARIMA provided an interpretable per-district baseline (median\nerror 8.5%).  The most critical societal finding is the gender\nviolence surge of +58.4% versus +18.6% total IPC growth—\na structural divergence demanding dedicated policy attention\naligned with SDG 16.1.   Poverty emerged as the dominant\nstructural driver (β = 33,340,p < 0.001), confirmed inde-\npendently by OLS and SHAP. The CRI (Eq. 1) operationalises\nthese findings into a deployable district triage tool, flagging 181\nDBSCAN-anomalous districts and identifying Dibang Valley,\nNarayanpur, Alirajpur, Jhabua, and Pratapgarh as the highest-\nrisk districts for 2012.\nLimitations: The dataset ends in 2012 and Census 2011 is\nstatic for all years.  No police capacity variable is included.\nDBSCAN is sensitive to the eps parameter. Results are observa-\ntional; causal inference requires instrumental variable methods.\nFuture work: LSTM/Transformer time-series models; real-\ntime NCRB integration; police station density as an additional\nfeature; causal inference; and satellite/mobile data fusion.\nACKNOWLEDGEMENT\nThe  authors  gratefully  acknowledge  the  National  Crime\nRecords Bureau (NCRB) of India for the IPC crime dataset and\nthe Office of the Registrar General & Census Commissioner of\nIndia for Census 2011 data available via data.gov.in.\nAPPENDIX I: FEATURE ENGINEERING FORMULAS\nliteracy\nrate = literate/population\nfemaleliteracy = femaleliterate/(female + 1)\nworkerratio = workers/population\nfemale\nworkerr = femaleworkers/(female + 1)\nagriworkerr = (cult. + agri.)/(workers + 1)\ncrimerate = totalipc/population× 100,000\nurbanratio = urbanHH/(HH + 1)\nscstratio = (SC + ST)/(population + 1)\nyouth\nratio = age\n0–29\n/(population + 1)\ninfraindex = (elec + latrine + tapwater)/(HH×3 + 1)\npovertyratio = pp\n<45k\n/(HH + 1)\nCRI Weight Rationale:\nComponentWeightRationale\nCrime rate (\n ̃\nC)0.40Primary outcome—direct in-\ntensity\n1−Literacy (\n ̃\nL)0.20Persistent structural risk\nPoverty (\n ̃\nP )0.20Strongest OLS predictor\nWorker ratio (\n ̃\nW )0.10Employment  as  protective\nfactor\n1−Infra (\n ̃\nI )0.10Infrastructure deficit ampli-\nfies risk\nREFERENCES\n[1]W. Gorr and R. Harries, “Introduction to crime forecasting,” Int.\nJ. Forecasting, vol. 19, no. 4, pp. 551–555, 2003.\n[2]S. Chainey, L. Tompson, and S. Uhlig, “The utility of hotspot\nmapping for predicting spatial patterns of crime,” Security J.,\nvol. 21, no. 1–2, pp. 4–28, 2008.\n[3]R. Iqbal, A. Murad, A. Mushtaq, P. Haque, and M. Shad, “An\nempirical study of classification algorithms for crime prediction,”\nJ. Intell. Learning Syst. Appl., vol. 5, no. 3, 2013.\n[4]W. Perry, B. McInnis, C. Price, S. Smith, and J. Hollywood,\nPredictive Policing: The Role of Crime Forecasting in Law\nEnforcement Operations. RAND Corporation, 2013.\n[5]L. McClendon and N. Meghanathan, “Using machine learning\nalgorithms to analyze crime data,” Machine Learning Appl.: Int.\nJ., vol. 2, no. 1, pp. 1–12, 2015.\n[6]P. Cozens and T. Love, “A review and current status of crime\nprevention through environmental design (CPTED),” J. Planning\nLiterature, vol. 30, no. 4, pp. 393–412, 2015.\n[7]A. Khanna and P. Bhatt, “Crime analysis on NCRB data using\ndata mining techniques,” in Proc. Int. Conf. Inventive Systems\nand Control (ICISC), 2017, pp. 1–5.\n[8]\nN. Kaur and S. Singh,  “Effect of socio-economic factors on\ncrime in India: A statistical analysis,” Int. J. Applied Soc. Sci.,\nvol. 5, no. 7–8, pp. 614–620, 2018.\n[9]\nT. Devi, A. Nandal, and S. Dhiman, “Predicting crime in Indian\nstates using K-Means clustering,” Int. J. Recent Technol. Eng.,\nvol. 8, no. 2S3, pp. 912–918, 2019.\n[10]\nS. Gupta and R. Sharma, “Trends in gender-based violence in\nIndia: An analysis of NCRB data 2010–2019,” Indian J. Crimi-\nnology, vol. 48, no. 1, pp. 23–39, 2020.\n[11]F. K. Bappee, A. Junior, and A. Matwin, “Predicting crime using\nspatial features,” in Proc. Canadian AI 2018, LNAI 10832, 2018,\npp. 367–373.\n[12]D.  Yi,  S.  Liu,  and  R.  Ji,  “Applying  SHAP  to  explain  crime\nprediction models,” IEEE Access, vol. 8, pp. 171169–171178,\n2020.\n[13]\nQ. Wu, H. Chen, and Z. Li, “Urban crime pattern detection using\ngradient boosting and DBSCAN,” Sustainability, vol. 13, no. 15,\np. 8426, 2021.\n[14]\nUnited Nations, “Transforming our world: The 2030 agenda for\nsustainable development,” A/RES/70/1, UN General Assembly,\n2015.\n[15]\nD. Sharma, K. Gupta, and M. Tiwari, “Panel data analysis of\ncrime determinants across Indian states,” J. Policy Modeling,\nvol. 43, no. 2, pp. 458–475, 2021.\n[16]L. E. Cohen and M. Felson, “Social change and crime rate trends:\nA routine activity approach,” American Sociol. Rev.,  vol. 44,\nno. 4, pp. 588–608, 1979.\n[17]R. K. Merton, “Social structure and anomie,” American Sociol.\nRev., vol. 3, no. 5, pp. 672–682, 1938.\n[18]C. R. Shaw and H. D. McKay, Juvenile Delinquency and Urban\nAreas. Univ. Chicago Press, 1942.\n[19]T. Chen and C. Guestrin, “XGBoost: A scalable tree boosting\nsystem,” in Proc. 22nd ACM SIGKDD, 2016, pp. 785–794.\n[20]L. Breiman, “Random forests,” Machine Learning, vol. 45, no. 1,\npp. 5–32, 2001.\n[21]S. M. Lundberg and S.-I. Lee, “A unified approach to interpreting\nmodel predictions,” in Advances in Neural Information Process-\ning Systems 30, 2017, pp. 4765–4774.\n5\n\n[22]M. Ester, H.-P. Kriegel, J. Sander, and X. Xu, “A density-based\nalgorithm for discovering clusters in large spatial databases,” in\nProc. 2nd Int. Conf. KDD, 1996, pp. 226–231.\n[23]J. MacQueen, “Some methods for classification and analysis of\nmultivariate observations,” in Proc. 5th Berkeley Symp. Math.\nStatist. Prob., 1967, pp. 281–297.\n[24]G. E. P. Box and G. M. Jenkins, Time Series Analysis: Forecast-\ning and Control. Holden-Day, 1970.\n[25]W. Skogan, Disorder and Decline: Crime and the Spiral of\nDecay in American Neighborhoods. Free Press, 1990.\n[26]S. D. Levitt, “Using electoral cycles in police hiring to estimate\nthe effect of police on crime,” American Econ. Rev., vol. 87,\nno. 3, pp. 270–290, 1997.\n[27]National Crime Records Bureau, Crime in India 2012. Ministry\nof Home Affairs, Government of India, New Delhi, 2013.\n[28]Office of the Registrar General & Census Commissioner, Census\nof India 2011: District-Level Data. Government of India, 2012.\n[Online]. Available: https://data.gov.in\n[29]F. Pedregosa et al., “Scikit-learn: Machine learning in Python,”\nJ. Machine Learning Research, vol. 12, pp. 2825–2830, 2011.\n[30]\nS.  Seabold  and  J.  Perktold,  “Statsmodels:  Econometric  and\nstatistical modeling with Python,” in Proc. 9th Python in Science\nConf., 2010, pp. 92–96.\n[31]A. A. Hagberg, D. A. Schult, and P. J. Swart, “Exploring network\nstructure, dynamics, and function using NetworkX,” in Proc. 7th\nPython in Science Conf., 2008, pp. 11–15.\n[32]J. Kleinberg, H. Lakkaraju, J. Leskovec, J. Ludwig, and S. Mul-\nlainathan, “Human decisions and machine predictions,” Q. J.\nEcon., vol. 133, no. 1, pp. 237–293, 2018.\n[33]W. D. Heaven, “Predictive policing algorithms are racist. They\nneed to be dismantled,” MIT Technology Review, Jul. 2020.\n[34]G. O. Mohler, M. B. Short, P. J. Brantingham, F. P. Schoenberg,\nand G. E. Tita, “Self-exciting point process modeling of crime,”\nJ. Amer. Statist. Assoc., vol. 106, no. 493, pp. 100–108, 2011.\n[35]B. Wang, Y. Zhang, and X. Wang, “A review of deep learning\nmethods applied to crime prediction,” IEEE Trans. Comput. Soc.\nSyst., vol. 6, no. 4, pp. 678–689, 2019.\n[36]N. Dash and B. Patra, “Machine learning-based crime prediction\nsystem for Indian districts using NCRB data,” J. Ambient Intell.\nHumanized Comput., vol. 13, pp. 5613–5626, 2022.\n[37]S. Misra and A. Singh, “Socio-economic correlates of crime in\nIndia: Evidence from panel data,” Econ. Political Weekly, vol. 57,\nno. 12, pp. 44–51, 2022.\n[38]UNICEF, “Violence against women and girls: A compendium\nof monitoring and evaluation indicators,” UNICEF, New York,\n2020.\n[39]\nUNDP, “Tackling social norms:  A game changer for gender\ninequalities,” Human Development Perspectives, UNDP, New\nYork, 2020.\n6','2026-07-26 09:29:59'),(17,19,'\n\nLab Assignment: Case Study on Use Case Diagram\nUseful References - \n1.What is a use case diagram? - Use case diagram\n2.Use case diagram explanation  \n3.Examples      \nTools -  StarUML , draw.io, SmartDraw\nCase Study 1: Library Management System\na. Prepare a detailed use case diagram for a Library Management System, including all actors, use \ncases, and their interactions (associations, include and extend relationships) as described by the case \nstudy given below. \nb. Identify the two major use cases and write the use case specifications for the same\nCase Study Description:\nDesign a Library Management System to simplify and automate how members, librarians, and \nadministrators interact with library resources. The primary goal of the system is to ensure efficient \nmanagement of books, users, and transactions such as issuing and returning books, while \nmaintaining accurate records and minimizing manual effort.\nThe system should support different user types: Members, who primarily search, borrow, and return \nbooks; Librarians, who manage books, monitor transactions, and calculate fines and Administrators, \nwho oversee user management and overall system operations. Each user interacts with the system \nthrough authentication mechanisms such as registration and login, and their access to functionalities \ndepends on their assigned role.\nMembers can search for books using various criteria such as title, author, or category and view \ndetailed information including availability status. Before a book is issued, the system must always \nverify the availability of the selected book to ensure that only available books can be borrowed. \nMembers can return borrowed books within the due date, and during every return operation, the \navailability status of the returned book in the library catalog must be updated.\nLibrarians are responsible for maintaining the library catalog. They can add new books, update \nbook information, remove damaged or outdated books, and manage member records. In case of \noverdue returns, librarians calculate the applicable fine based on library policies, and members can \nsubsequently view and pay the fine through the system.\nAdministrators have higher-level control over the system. They can manage librarian accounts, \ngenerate usage reports, monitor system performance, and ensure smooth operation of all library \nactivities.\n\nCase Study 2: Online Hospital Appointment & Care Management\na. Prepare a detailed use case diagram for Online Hospital Appointment & Care Management \nSystem, including all actors, use cases, and their interactions (associations, include and extend \nrelationships) as described by the case study given below. \nb. Identify the two major use cases and write the use case specifications for the same\nCase Study Description:\nImagine a hospital where patients no longer need to stand in long queues just to book an \nappointment or collect reports. The Online Hospital Appointment & Care Management System \nprovides a digital platform that connects patients, doctors, hospital staff, and administrators in one \nintegrated system.\nPatients can either log in using an existing account or register as new users. Once logged in, they \ncan search for doctors based on specialization, availability, or department. Patients can book \nappointments at their preferred time slots, reschedule or cancel appointments if their plans change, \nand view their appointment history. After consultation, patients can view prescriptions, download \nmedical reports, and raise queries or complaints if issues arise regarding services or billing.\nDoctors use the system to manage their daily schedules, view upcoming appointments, access \npatient medical history, and update diagnoses and prescriptions after consultations. Lab technicians \nupload test results, which are securely linked to patient profiles.\nHospital administrators ensure smooth operation behind the scenes. They manage doctor \nonboarding, department creation, appointment slots, user accounts, and monitor complaints. The \nsystem also integrates with an external notification service to send appointment reminders, \nprescription alerts, and report availability updates to patients.\nOverall, the system improves efficiency, reduces waiting time, and ensures seamless \ncommunication between patients and hospital staff.','2026-07-29 03:11:46'),(18,20,'\n\nExplore higher education and compare university performance on sustainability:\nhttps://www.timeshighereducation.com/impactrankings\nThe Client’s (aka DUU’s) Impact Rankings:\nAbout DUU:\nDown Under University (DUU) offers hundreds of undergraduate and\npostgraduate study options that include extensive research programs. Our\nleading academics, state-of the-art-teaching facilities and a focussed approach to\nlearning delivers real-world outcomes for students and graduates.\nOur 7,000 staff and 50,000 students come from many different cultural and\nreligious backgrounds, including international students from over 140 countries.','2026-07-29 03:12:41'),(19,21,'\n\nProblem Statement - Student Management System\nThe application should be a web-based Student Management System that performs \nbasic CRUD operations using:\nSpring Boot (MVC Architecture)\nHibernate (JPA)\nRelational Database (MySQL / H2)\n1️ . Home Page (Main Screen)\nWhen the application runs (http://localhost:8080/), the browser should display:\nStudent Registration Form\nThe page must contain a form with:\nName (Text field)\nEmail (Text field)\nCourse (Text field or dropdown)\nSubmit button\nFunctionality:\nWhen user enters details and clicks Submit button,\n→ Data must be stored in the database\n→ Page must refresh \n→ Newly added student must appear in list below\nStudent List Table\nBelow the form, the page must display a table containing:\nIDNameEmailCourse\nRequirements:\nData must be fetched from database\nTable must update after each insert\nID must be auto-generated\n\nImplementation Details\n1️. Model\nThe Model should define the following classes and methods:\n Class: Student\nRepresents a student record in the system with the following attributes:\nid (Auto-generated unique identifier)\nname\nemail\ncourse\nCRUD Operations for Student\nCreate:\nAdd a new student record when a user submits the registration form.\nRead:\nRetrieve student by ID\nRetrieve all students\nRetrieve students by course\n2. View (User Interface)\nCreate web page to allow users to:\nSubmit a new student registration form (all fields required).\nView all student records in a list/table format.\n3. Controller\nThe StudentController class is responsible for handling user interactions related to student records.\nIt acts as an intermediary between the frontend (View) and backend (Model) by processing HTTP \nrequests and interacting with the StudentService.\n\nThe controller provides the following RESTful endpoints:\nPOST /api/students\nCreates a new student record based on user input.\nGET /api/students\nFetches a list of all student records.\nResponsibilities-\nThe controller must:\nValidate incoming requests.\nEnsure required fields are present.\nInteract with the service layer for business logic.\nPersist data using JPA/Hibernate.\n4. Service Layer Responsibilities\nThe StudentService should:\nContain business logic.\nPrevent duplicate email registration.\nCoordinate with repository.\n Expected Functional Behavior\n Student can be added✔\n Student list can be viewed✔\n Duplicate student details not allowed.✔\n Data persists in database✔\n MVC structure is properly followed✔','2026-07-29 03:13:07');
